        "subtitle": "In this upside-down world, selling a dress at a huge discount turns out to be very good business indeed, says William Langley",
        "text": "Since a good year in the haute couture business is one in which you lose even more money than usual, the general mood in Paris last week was one of optimism. The big-name designers were competing to boast about how many outfits they had sold below cost price, and how this proved that the fashion business was healthier than ever. Jean-Paul Gaultier reported record sales, \"but we don't make any money out of it,\" the designer assured journalists backstage. \"No matter how successful you are, you can't make a profit from couture,\" explained Jean-Jacques Picart, a long-serving fashion PR man and co-founder of the now bankrupt Lacroix house.\n\nAlmost 20 years have passed since the strange economics of the couture business were first revealed. Furious that he was losing money on evening dresses costing tens of thousands of pounds, the couturier Jean-Louis Scherrer published a detailed summary of his costs, to howls of \"treason\" from his colleagues. One outfit he described contained over half a mile of gold thread and 18,000 sequins, and had needed hundreds of hours of hand-stitching in a workshop. A fair price would have been £50,000, but the couturier could only get £35,000 for it. Far from growing rich on the follies of the super-rich, he and his team could barely feed their hungry families.\n\nThe result was an outcry and the first of a series of inquiries, sponsored by the government and the industry, into the unreal world of ultimate fashion. The trade still insists that, relatively speaking, couture gives you more than you pay for, but it is not as simple as that. When such a temple of old wealth starts talking about value for money, it is not trying to convince anyone that dresses costing as much as houses are a bargain. Rather, it is trying to preserve the peculiar mystique, the lucrative associations and the threatened interests that couture represents.\n\nBasically, the arguments could not be simpler. On one side are those who say that the business will die if it doesn't change. On the other are those who say it will die if it does. What is not in doubt is that haute couture (the term means \"high sewing\") is a spectacular anachronism. Colossal in its costs, tiny in its clientele and questionable in its influence, it still remains one of the great themes of Parisian life. In his book The Fashion Conspiracy, Nicholas Coleridge estimates that the whole couture industry depends on the whims of fewer than 30 extremely wealthy women, and although the number may have grown in recent years with Asia's new prosperity, there are no more than 4,000 couture customers worldwide.\n\nTo count as couture, a garment must be made entirely by hand by one of the 11 Paris couture houses registered with the Chambre Syndicale de la Haute Couture. Each house must employ at least 20 people and show at least 75 new designs a year. So far, so traditional, but the Big Four operators (Chanel, Dior, Givenchy and Gaultier) increasingly use couture as a marketing tool for their far more profitable ready-to-wear, fragrance and accessory lines.\n\nIt is not hard to see how this works in practice. \"Haute couture is what gives our business its essential essence of luxury,\" says Bernard Arnault, the head of LVMH, which owns both Dior and Givenchy. \"The cash it soaks up is largely irrelevant. Against the money we lose, you have to set the value of the image couture gives us. Look at the attention the collections attract. It is where you get noticed. You have to be there. It's where we set our ideas in motion.\"\n\nThe big idea is the one known in the trade as \"name association\". Couture outfits may be unaffordable, even unwearable, but the hint of glamour and exclusivity is hard to resist. The busy modern woman who does not earn enough in a year to buy a single piece of couture can still buy a share of the dream for the price of a Chanel lipstick or a Givenchy scarf.\n\nEven so, couture has been in decline for years (the optimists would say it has been adjusting to changed conditions). The number of houses registered with the Syndicale has halved in the last two decades. Pierre Cardin once had almost 500 people working full time on couture; by the 1980s the number had fallen to 50, and today the house is no longer registered.\n\nModern life tells the story. Younger women, even the seriously wealthy ones, find ready-to-wear clothes always more practical and usually more fun, and couture's market has shrunk. \"Haute couture is a joke,\" says Pierre Bergé scornfully; he is the former head of Yves Saint Laurent, another house that no longer creates it. \"Anyone who tells you it still matters is fantasising. You can see it dropping dead all around you. Nobody buys it any more. The prices are ridiculous. The rules for making it are nonsensical. It belongs to another age. Where are today's couturiers? A real couturier is someone who founds and runs their own house. No one does that any more.\"\n\nSo why are the surviving couture houses smiling? Because they trade in fantasy, and in times like these more people want to fantasise. \"We've received so many orders we may not be able to deliver them all,\" says Sidney Toledano, the head of Dior. So the clothes are rolled out, the couture losses roll in, and everyone agrees that it is good business."
